From d9ca10bec15168e64bdf0bc18af7e84ead63e4b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: mt7621 model <model@local>
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 19:55:01 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] mt7621: 1004Kc MT bring-up; CP0 compare via GIC local timer

The 1004Kc core is 2 VPEs x 1 TC, and Linux (CONFIG_MIPS_MT_SMP + CPS)
brings the second VPE of each core up with cps-vec's mttc0 sequence.  Three
core-model details are needed for that to work on a multi-core part:

- VPECONTROL.TARGTC numbers TCs within the processor (core), not globally.
  mips_cpu_map_tc() assumed a global thread index, so from core 1 it
  addressed core 0's VPEs.  When the machine describes the topology (one
  CPUState per VPE, nr_threads VPs per core) map TARGTC to the sibling VCPU
  of the current core.

- The translation-time gate in gen_mftr()/gen_mttr() NOPs when
  TARGTC > MVPConf0.PTC.  On a 1004K PTC is 0 (one TC per VPE) while TARGTC
  is 1 for the second VP of the core, so every sibling access became a NOP.
  Ask instead whether the TC exists in the processor and whether the target
  is another VPE of this core.

- EVP is per processor: DVPE/EVPE must only stop/start the other VPEs of
  the executing core.  Iterating over every CPU stopped the running boot
  core whenever a secondary core was released.

Route the CP0 count/compare interrupt through the GIC on a CM part.  On a
1004Kc covered by a Coherence Manager the compare interrupt is the GIC's
VP-local GIC_LOCAL_INT_TIMER (2), latched in GIC_VP_PEND and forwarded only
when the VP mask bit and GIC_VPE_TIMER_MAP say so.  The model raised
Cause.IP7 directly, which is the pre-CM wiring and made an unhandled
"irq 7: nobody cared" storm on every guest that never routes the local
timer (the board shows no irq 7 activity at all).  A weak
cpu_mips_timer_irq() keeps the old default for machines without a CM; the
CM machine overrides it, and the GIC now models local 2 (mask, map,
pending) alongside its own compare.

GIC_VPE_CTL must report TIMER_ROUTABLE (bit 1): gic_get_c0_compare_int()
uses it to decide between the GIC local timer and CPU IP7.  Reporting 0 put
a guest that registers the r4k clockevent (RouterOS sets mips_hpt_frequency)
on IP7 while cpu_mips_timer_irq() delivered the compare as local 2 - a line
nobody acknowledged, held high (giclevel: local 2 held high) and eventually
wedging a CPU.

Also model the CP0 compare write race: a guest arms Compare from a Count it
has just read, and host call latency can make a small delta arrive already
behind the count.  The wrap-around interpretation then schedules the compare
2^32 ticks later, which is what Linux's c0_compare_int_usable() (50-tick
window) rejects.  A fresh arm (no pending Cause.TI) that is at most 10 us
behind expires immediately; an acknowledgment keeps the wrap semantics.

MT7621_GICIRQLOG reports per-source interrupt rates (gicrate) and held-high
levels (giclevel), so an unacknowledged level that storms a CPU is visible
without a per-transition flood.
